---
<Base updated="2026-11-23" title="Home Battery Cost in Indiana (2026): $13,000 Average | HomeBackupCost" description="A 13 kWh home battery averages $13,000 installed in Indiana ($1,000/kWh, EnergySage, Nov 2026). Costs by size, rebate status, outage data, and cheaper options." schema={[{ '@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'FAQPage', mainEntity: faq.map(([q,a]) => ({ '@type':'Question', name:q, acceptedAnswer:{ '@type':'Answer', text:a } })) }]}>
<h1>How much does a home battery cost in Indiana?</h1>
<p class="lede"><strong>Short answer:</strong> about <strong>$13,000</strong> for a 13 kWh battery installed, before incentives, according to EnergySage marketplace data for Indiana. That's about $1,000 per kWh, below the $1,159/kWh national average. EnergySage's older first-half-2026 guide put Indiana higher ($1,333/kWh), so treat $13,000 as a fair-price target, not a guarantee. As of 2026-11-23.</p>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><div class="mute">Indiana average (13 kWh)</div><div style="font-size:2.2rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--volt)">$13,000</div><div class="src"><a href="https://www.energysage.com/local-data/energy-storage-cost/in/">EnergySage</a> · updated 2026-11-15</div></div>
<div class="card"><div class="mute">Indiana per kWh</div><div style="font-size:2.2rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--volt)">$1,000</div><div class="src">National: $1,159/kWh (<a href="https://www.energysage.com/energy-storage/">EnergySage</a> · updated 2026-08-26)</div></div>
<div class="card"><div class="mute">Indiana battery rebate</div><div style="font-size:2.2rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--bad)">None found</div><div class="src"><a href="https://indianaenergysaver.com/programs/home-appliance-rebate/">Indiana Energy Saver</a> list has no batteries (checked 2026-11-23)</div></div>
</div>

<h2>Indiana cost by battery size</h2>
<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>System size</th><th>Average installed cost</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>10 kWh</td><td>$10,000</td></tr><tr><td>13 kWh</td><td>$13,000</td></tr><tr><td>20 kWh</td><td>$20,000</td></tr><tr><td>26 kWh</td><td>$26,000</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="src">Source: <a href="https://www.energysage.com/local-data/energy-storage-cost/in/">EnergySage, Storage cost in Indiana</a>, updated 2026-11-15, checked 2026-11-23. These are quotes on the EnergySage marketplace, before incentives. The math is size × $1,000/kWh: 13 × $1,000 = $13,000. EnergySage gives a 13 kWh range of $11,050–$14,950, and $17,740–$51,800 for solar plus storage. Top quoted models there: HomeGrid Stack'd Series 19.2 kWh, FranklinWH aPower S-10, Tesla Powerwall 3, and Enphase IQ Battery 5P. EnergySage's separate <a href="https://www.energysage.com/energy-storage/how-much-do-batteries-cost/">cost guide</a> (updated 2026-07-13) lists Indiana at $1,333/kWh for a typical 13.5 kWh system ($17,996) from first-half-2026 quotes. The two EnergySage figures are far apart; the local page is the newer one.</p>

<h2>Incentives in Indiana</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Federal tax credit:</strong> ended for installs completed after 12/31/2025 (<a href="/federal-tax-credits-ended/">details</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Indiana Energy Saver Program, Home Appliance Rebate (HEAR):</strong> open to households under 150% of area median income, up to $14,000 per household. Eligible items are heat pumps (up to $8,000), heat pump water heaters ($1,750), heat pump dryers ($840), electric stoves ($840), electrical panels ($4,000), wiring ($2,500), and insulation and air sealing ($1,600). Batteries are not on the list, and the rebates can't replace existing gas equipment (<a href="https://indianaenergysaver.com/programs/home-appliance-rebate/">Indiana Energy Saver</a>, checked 2026-11-23).</li>
<li><strong>NASEO tracker:</strong> lists Indiana HEAR and HOMES as launched (<a href="https://www.naseo.org/topics/home-energy-rebates">NASEO</a>, table updated 2026-09-30).</li>
</ul>

<h2>How often Indiana loses power</h2>
<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Indiana, average customer</th><th>2025</th><th>2024</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Minutes without power, all events</td><td>374.0</td><td>312.4</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minutes without power, excluding major events</td><td>123.2</td><td>121.4</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interruptions per year, all events</td><td>1.390</td><td>1.283</td></tr>
<tr><td>U.S. total, minutes without power, all events</td><td>262.6</td><td>611.3</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="src">Source: <a href="https://www.eia.gov/electricity/annual/html/epa_11_03.html">EIA Electric Power Annual, Table 11.3</a> (SAIDI and SAIFI), checked 2026-11-23. 374 ÷ 60 = 6.2 hours; 374 ÷ 262.6 = 1.4 times the U.S. figure. Most of Indiana's 2025 total came from major events (374 − 123.2 = 250.8 minutes).</p>

<h2>What it costs to recharge</h2>
<p>Refilling a 13 kWh battery from the grid costs about <strong>$2.21</strong> at Indiana's average residential price of 17.02¢/kWh in August 2026 (U.S. average 18.16¢). The math: 13 × $0.1702 = $2.21, before charging losses. Source: <a href="https://www.eia.gov/electricity/monthly/epm_table_grapher.php?t=epmt_5_6_a">EIA Electric Power Monthly, Table 5.6.A</a>, checked 2026-11-23.</p>

<h2>Cheaper options</h2>
<p>If you mainly need the fridge, furnace blower, sump pump, and Wi-Fi through a short outage, a large portable station with a transfer kit costs much less. For example, an <ProductLink product="EcoFlow DELTA Pro 3" bold={false} button={false} /> ($2,799 sale, standalone unavailable on Nov 16, 2026) plus a Smart Home Panel 2 ($1,499 at ecoflow.com, in stock on Nov 16, 2026) is $4,298 before electrician labor. See <a href="/ecoflow-vs-jackery/">EcoFlow vs Jackery</a>, <a href="/portable-power-stations/">all power stations</a>, <a href="/generac-vs-home-battery/">Generac vs a home battery</a>, and <a href="/standby-generator-installation-cost/">standby generator cost</a>. Sump pump in the basement? See <a href="/how-long-will-a-power-station-run-a-sump-pump/">sump pump runtime</a>.</p>
<p><a class="btn" href="/calculator/">Size your battery in the calculator</a></p>
<p>Comparing quotes? Use <a href="/how-to-compare-backup-power-quotes/">how to compare quotes</a> and <a href="/questions-to-ask-backup-power-installer/">questions to ask an installer</a>. Neighbors: <a href="/ohio-home-battery-cost/">Ohio</a> · <a href="/michigan-home-battery-cost/">Michigan</a> · <a href="/pennsylvania-home-battery-cost/">Pennsylvania</a>.</p>
